<h2 class="heading-size-2 heading-bar" id="set-bonuses">
<span data-markup-content-target="1">
<span class="iconmedium" data-color="c1" data-contiguous="true" data-env="mists" data-game="wow" data-kind="round" data-tree="mists">
<ins class="lazy-load-background" style='background-image: url("https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/medium/inv_misc_treasurechest02b.jpg");'></ins>
<del></del></span> Tier &amp; Set Bonuses for Protection Warriors in Mists of Pandaria Classic</span></h2>Tier 15 has some very strong bonuses, and the <span class="icontiny lazy-load-background" data-contiguous="true" style='background-image: url("https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/class_warrior.gif");'></span>
<span class="c1">Protection Warrior</span> ones are good, but not great. In a full BIS set, you do pursue a 4-set bonus, regardless of whether you're going for a balanced or more offensive build, but it's up to you and your raid whether you'd like to prioritize the tier tokens on other members of your raid before you get yours. <br/>
<br/>We have two options to choose from, and they both bring solid utility. <br/>
<br/>The tank tier 2-set allows you to use Victory Rush when you get a proc after using Shield Slam or Revenge, giving the Protection Warrior some much-needed self-sustain. This set bonus has limited utility in a 25-man environment, but is a great addition to your toolkit if you're raiding 10-man. <br/>
<br/>The tank tier 4-set looks slightly better on paper than in reality. In most realistic scenarios, we'll use <a class="icontiny" data-contiguous="true" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/spell=122013/glyph-of-incite">
<img alt="" loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/ability_warrior_incite.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Glyph of Incite</span></a>, meaning we don't have the ability to dump our excess rage efficiently. You can still dump excess Rage into <a class="icontiny" data-contiguous="true" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/spell=112048/shield-barrier">
<img alt="" loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/inv_shield_07.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Shield Barrier</span></a>, assuming you have <a class="icontiny" data-contiguous="true" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/spell=2565/shield-block">
<img alt="" loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/ability_defend.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Shield Block</span></a> active already-- but even then you'll likely overcap on Rage as the boss is unlikely to break the Shield Barrier with Shield Block and Demoralizing Shout active. That said, I'm still in favor of using the set, especially as most of the pieces are well itemized for the Protection Warrior. <br/>
<br/>The DPS set is quite attractive for Protection Warriors in Phase 3, especially the 2-set, which directly translates into additional Rage and damage through <a class="icontiny" data-contiguous="true" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/spell=12880/enrage">
<img alt="" loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_shadow_unholyfrenzy.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Enrage</span></a>. The set will increase your Enrage procs by approximately 20-25%. <br/>
<br/>The 4-set was changed to not give its bonus while in Defensive Stance, and as Protection Warriors lose their Vengeance while leaving Defensive Stance, this set bonus is effectively useless for Protection Warriors.<h3 class="heading-size-3 heading-bar" id="tier-15-tank">
<span data-markup-content-target="1">Tier 15 (Tank) - <a data-entity="item-set" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/item-set=1173/plate-of-the-last-mogu">
<span class="tinyicontxt">Plate of the Last Mogu</span></a></span></h3>
